    "type": "question",
    "label": "3",
    "prompt": "Show that $3\\log_{10}x+2\\log_{10}y-\\frac12\\log_{10}z=\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{x^3y^2}{\\sqrt z}\\right)$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\log_{10}\\left(\\dfrac{x^3y^2}{\\sqrt z}\\right)$, as required.\n\nApply the power law, then combine using the product and quotient laws.",
      "worked": "For the logarithms to be defined, $x,y,z>0$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}3\\log_{10}x+2\\log_{10}y-\\tfrac12\\log_{10}z&=\\log_{10}(x^3)+\\log_{10}(y^2)-\\log_{10}(z^{1/2})\\\\3\\log_{10}x+2\\log_{10}y-\\tfrac12\\log_{10}z&=\\log_{10}(x^3y^2)-\\log_{10}(z^{1/2})\\\\3\\log_{10}x+2\\log_{10}y-\\tfrac12\\log_{10}z&=\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{x^3y^2}{z^{1/2}}\\right)\\\\3\\log_{10}x+2\\log_{10}y-\\tfrac12\\log_{10}z&=\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{x^3y^2}{\\sqrt z}\\right).\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
